(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-2-exam-1-ent-exam-quiz"]={"title":"Advanced ENT History & Examination Quiz 1 — PD2 Exam 1","category":"Physical Diagnosis","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What are the four Centor criteria?","choices":["Fever, tonsillar exudates, swollen anterior cervical adenopathy, and ABSENCE of cough","Fever, tonsillar exudates, swollen anterior cervical adenopathy, and PRESENCE of cough","Fever, sore throat, headache and myalgia","Fever, trismus, uvular deviation and drooling"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Three are things present and one is a thing absent, which is the part most often misremembered. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Amber fluid behind the tympanic membrane, sometimes with visible bubbles. What is this finding?","choices":["Blood behind the membrane from temporal bone fracture","Cerumen visible through a perforation","Serous effusion","Purulent effusion from acute otitis media"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A serous effusion is amber fluid behind the eardrum, sometimes with bubbles, brought on by an upper respiratory infection or a change in atmospheric pressure. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A patient reports ear pain but the ear itself looks entirely normal. Which structures should the history now cover?","choices":["The jaw joint, the teeth and the cervical spine","The frontal and maxillary sinuses","The thyroid gland and the trachea","The parotid and submandibular salivary glands"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ear pain is frequently referred, and these three are the usual sources — the first answer a patient gives is not the last question you ask. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which epistaxis history suggests a SYSTEMIC problem rather than a local one?","choices":["Bleeding from one nostril after digital trauma","Bleeding that follows a recent upper respiratory infection","Bleeding in dry winter air","Recurrent bleeding, or bleeding and bruising elsewhere on the body"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. One nosebleed is local until proven otherwise; a pattern of bleeding at other sites changes the question entirely. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A smooth, beefy red tongue points to which deficiency?","choices":["Vitamin D","Vitamin B12","Vitamin C","Iron alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The appearance is atrophic glossitis, and it earns a look for the rest of the deficiency picture. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"How does the course of CENTRAL vertigo differ in duration from the peripheral causes?","choices":["Variable, but rarely continuous","Always continuous until treated","Always under one minute","Always several hours"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Central vertigo runs a variable course but is rarely continuous, whereas each peripheral cause has a characteristic duration. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"How is the auricle positioned to straighten the ear canal for otoscopy in an adult?","choices":["Pull it down and back","Press it forward against the cheek","Leave it undisturbed and advance the speculum slowly","Pull it up, back, and away from the head"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — all three directions. The canal is not straight, and the view is only as good as the alignment. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What is microtia?","choices":["An extra accessory auricle","A small pit anterior to the tragus","An abnormally small auricle","An absent or closed ear canal"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Microtia is a congenitally underdeveloped, abnormally small auricle, and it is grouped with atresia among the congenital deformities. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"How is the RINNE test performed, in order?","choices":["Fork on the vertex of the head, then moved to the mastoid","Fork held in front of both ears simultaneously","Fork on the mastoid until the sound stops, then beside the canal","Fork close to the canal first, then on the mastoid once the sound stops"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Bone conduction comes first, with the fork on the mastoid until the sound stops; the fork then moves close to the canal and the patient is asked if it is still heard. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which statement about discharge in acute sinusitis is correct?","choices":["Green discharge is diagnostic of bacterial infection","Clear discharge excludes sinusitis","Discharge is absent in acute sinusitis","Purulent discharge is suggestive"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the distinction matters — patients and clinicians both over-read green discharge as proof of bacterial infection. But the COLOR of the discharge is not diagnostic. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What does “disequilibrium” mean, as distinct from vertigo and presyncope?","choices":["Anxiety-driven dizziness","Unsteadiness or imbalance","A spinning sensation","Feeling faint or lightheaded"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Disequilibrium is unsteadiness or imbalance, without the spinning of vertigo or the faint, lightheaded feeling of presyncope. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"FINE hair and COARSE hair on examination point to which conditions?","choices":["Fine hair to hypothyroidism; coarse hair to hyperthyroidism","Both to hypothyroidism","Both to nutritional deficiency","Fine hair to hyperthyroidism; coarse hair to hypothyroidism"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — a small finding, but it is examined for at the same moment as the scalp and the skull. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"An abnormally ENLARGED head raises which two conditions?","choices":["Hydrocephalus and Paget disease","Microcephaly and hydrocephalus","Hypothyroidism and acromegaly","Paget disease and microcephaly"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. One expands the skull from within, the other thickens the bone itself. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"Why should recent dental work be asked about when a patient presents with facial pain?","choices":["Dental infection is the commonest cause of frontal sinusitis","Dental work may affect the maxillary sinuses","Dental anesthetic commonly causes anosmia","Dental work is a recognized cause of epistaxis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The maxillary sinus floor sits directly above the upper tooth roots, so a dental procedure can present as sinus pain. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Where is the fork placed for the WEBER test?","choices":["Just outside the ear canal, to test air conduction alone","Behind the ear on the occiput, to test both ears simultaneously","On the top of the head or mid-forehead","On the mastoid process, to compare bone against air conduction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The base of the fork sits on the top of the head or mid-forehead, and the test evaluates unilateral hearing loss by lateralization. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"How is transillumination of the MAXILLARY sinus performed?","choices":["In a dark room, shine the light down below the inner eye corner","In a dark room, shine the light up under the brow close to the nose","In a bright room, shine the light through the cheek from the side","Shine the light into the nostril and look at the forehead"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In a dark room the light shines down just below the inner corner of the eye, with the patient's mouth open. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which description of exostoses is correct?","choices":["Cholesterol deposits in the middle ear","Benign bony growths in the ear canal, causing conductive loss","Benign bony growths on the ossicles, causing sensorineural loss","Malignant bone tumors of the temporal bone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. They narrow the canal mechanically, which is why the loss is conductive. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A firm raised lesion at an old ear piercing site. Which description fits?","choices":["Basal cell carcinoma — more common in darker-skinned individuals","A keloid — scar tissue, more common in darker-skinned individuals","Tophi — and there is no skin-type association","A cutaneous cyst — more common in darker-skinned individuals"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Keloids are commonest on the shoulders and upper chest but arise at piercing sites, which puts them on the ear. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Nasal polyps are associated with which conditions?","choices":["Asthma, aspirin sensitivity and cystic fibrosis","Hypertension, anticoagulation and atherosclerosis","Smoking, alcohol and reflux","Diabetes, obesity and sleep apnea"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nasal polyps are associated with allergic rhinitis, aspirin sensitivity, asthma, chronic sinus infection and cystic fibrosis. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Under what symptom duration is acute BACTERIAL sinusitis unlikely?","choices":["Less than four weeks","Less than twelve weeks","Less than seven days","Less than three days"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Most cases under a week are viral, which is the single most useful piece of timing in the nose section. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What accompanies the vertigo of Ménière disease, beyond the vertigo itself?","choices":["Dysarthria and ataxia","Pressure or fullness in the affected ear","Nystagmus alone, with normal hearing","Impaired hearing on one side with cranial nerve V and VII involvement"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Ménière disease brings pressure or fullness in the affected ear, with fluctuating tinnitus and a sensorineural loss that recurs and eventually progresses. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What is a NORMAL Rinne result?","choices":["Bone conduction heard longer than air conduction","Both heard for exactly the same duration","Neither heard, in a quiet room","Air conduction heard longer than bone conduction"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Normally sound is heard longer through air than through bone, so air conduction outlasts bone conduction. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"At what age do conductive and sensorineural hearing loss typically begin?","choices":["Both typically begin in childhood","Conductive up to about forty; sensorineural in later years","Conductive in middle or later years; sensorineural in childhood","Both typically begin after sixty"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Conductive loss usually begins in childhood and young adulthood, up to age 40, and sensorineural loss in middle or later years. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which viral infections cause SENSORINEURAL hearing loss?","choices":["Rubella and cytomegalovirus","Rhinovirus and influenza","Respiratory syncytial virus and adenovirus","Herpes simplex and varicella zoster"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — both damage the inner ear, and both matter most when the exposure was congenital. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which part of the examiner's hand should rest against the patient during otoscopy?","choices":["The palm, to steady the patient's head","The thumb, to hold the auricle in position","No part — the otoscope is held clear of the patient","The ulnar aspect"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The ulnar aspect of the hand contacts the patient, anchoring the otoscope so a sudden movement does not drive it into the canal. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Vertigo comes on when the patient rolls onto one side in bed, lasts under a minute, and hearing is unaffected. Which is it?","choices":["Benign positional vertigo","Ménière disease","Vestibular neuronitis","Acoustic neuroma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Sudden onset on rolling onto the affected side or tilting the head up, a few seconds to under a minute, hearing not affected, tinnitus absent. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A MULTINODULAR enlarged thyroid carries a risk of malignancy under what circumstance?","choices":["Only if the gland is tender","Only in patients under thirty","When there is a family history","Always, regardless of history"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the multinodular gland is usually a metabolic process, and family history is what raises the concern. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"Which three findings together describe the tympanic membrane in acute otitis media?","choices":["Pale, thickened and immobile","Amber with visible bubbles","Red, bulging, and with the landmarks lost","Red, retracted, and with the landmarks exaggerated"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The purulent effusion behind it pushes it outward and obscures the normal anatomy. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A chalky white patch on the tympanic membrane of a patient who once had tympanostomy tubes. Which interpretation is correct?","choices":["Tympanosclerosis — and it always requires referral","A cholesteatoma — and it requires urgent referral","A healing perforation — which will close spontaneously","Tympanosclerosis — usually not significant"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Tympanosclerosis is a hyaline deposit in the membrane after severe otitis media or a healed perforation, and it is usually not clinically significant. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Submandibular swelling and erythema with cellulitis of the floor of the mouth. Which description fits?","choices":["A submandibular stone — the danger is infection","Ludwig's angina — it is life threatening because of the AIRWAY","Ludwig's angina — the danger is spread to the mediastinum only","A peritonsillar abscess — the danger is rupture"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The swelling pushes the tongue and floor of mouth upward and back, and the airway is the reason this cannot wait. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 82"}]};
